---
import { version } from '../../../package.json';
import CopyCommand from '../components/CopyCommand.astro';
import Feature from '../components/Feature.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Stats from '../components/Stats.astro';
import Steps from '../components/Steps.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { HOME } from '../site';

const repo = 'https://github.com/scmmishra/clue';
const install = 'curl -fsSL https://clue.shivam.dev/install | bash';
const { title, description } = HOME;

// Tells search engines that this page is about a piece of software.
const schema = {
	'@context': 'https://schema.org',
	'@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
	name: 'clue',
	description,
	url: Astro.site?.href,
	applicationCategory: 'DeveloperApplication',
	operatingSystem: 'macOS, Linux',
	softwareVersion: version,
	license: 'https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0',
	codeRepository: repo,
	offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
};

// Medians from benchmarks/README.md.
const waits = [
	{ value: '7.5', unit: 'ms', label: 'Start to a ready prompt' },
	{ value: '13.8', unit: 'ms', label: 'Resume to your last session' },
	{ value: '5.4', unit: 'ms', label: "First token, clue's time" },
];

const steps = [
	{ title: 'Install', text: 'macOS and Linux, on arm64 and x86_64.', command: install },
	{ title: 'Sign in', text: 'Save an API key. clue also reads ANTHROPIC_API_KEY from the environment.', command: 'clue login' },
	{ title: 'Run it', text: 'Start clue in your project and type what you want done.', command: 'clue' },
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} schema={schema}>
	<main class="home">
		<div class="hero">
			<div class="hero-text">
				<h1>A fast, simple coding agent for Claude</h1>
				<p class="lede">
					clue calls Claude's API through the API SDK, not the Agent SDK, so it bills per token. <code>clue usage</code> shows
					what it has cost.
				</p>
				<CopyCommand command={install} shortcut="c" />
				<p class="meta">v{version} (<a href="/changelog">notes</a>) · macOS and Linux · actively developed, so things may change</p>
			</div>

			<figure class="window">
					<figcaption class="bar">
						<span class="lights" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
						<span>~/code/app</span>
					</figcaption>
					<pre class="terminal" tabindex="0" aria-label="A clue session that fixes a flaky spec"><span class="dim">$</span> clue
<span class="dim">clue {version} · /help for commands</span>

<span class="mark">┃</span> the conversation spec is flaky, fix it

  I'll run the spec a few times to see how it fails.

<span class="mark">●</span> <span class="soft">3 tool calls · 1 command · 1 read · 1 search</span>
<span class="dim">├</span> <span class="soft">Ran bundle exec rspec spec/models/conversation_spec.rb</span>
<span class="dim">├</span> <span class="soft">Read spec/models/conversation_spec.rb  180 lines</span>
<span class="dim">└</span> <span class="soft">Searched "last_activity_at"  12 matches</span>

  The spec compares `last_activity_at` with `Time.now`. The two
  timestamps can land in different seconds. I'll freeze time in that
  example.

<span class="mark">●</span> <span class="soft">2 tool calls · 1 edit · 1 command</span>
<span class="dim">├</span> <span class="soft">Edited spec/models/conversation_spec.rb</span>  <span class="add">+3</span> <span class="remove">−1</span>
<span class="dim">└</span> <span class="soft">Ran bundle exec rspec spec/models/conversation_spec.rb</span>

  Fixed. The example now freezes time, and 5 runs in a row pass.

  <span class="dim">41s · $0.21 · 48k in · 1.2k out</span>

<span class="mark">┃</span>

<span class="dim">auto · opus 5.5 · medium                               ~/code/app (main)</span></pre>
				</figure>
		</div>

		<Section label={'Three waits,\nmeasured'}>
			<Stats items={waits} />
			<p class="note">
				Medians on one Apple silicon Mac. The first-token figure uses a local stand-in for the API, so it shows only
				clue's own time. <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/benchmarks/README.md`}>benchmarks</a>
			</p>
		</Section>

		<Section label="Fast">
			<div class="features">
				<Feature title="You can type right away">
					clue shows the prompt first. The SDK, MCP servers and skills load in the background.
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Resuming restores your scrollback">
					<code>clue -c</code> prints the conversation back into your terminal, so you can scroll up through it. When you
					start typing, clue sends the history to Claude, so its cache is ready when you press enter.
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="The same system prompt every time">
					The system prompt and tool list are identical in every session, so Claude reads them from its cache.
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Long sessions send only what's new">
					Each request carries the messages since Claude's last reply. Claude's API keeps the rest of the conversation for
					a few minutes.
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Reads start while Claude is still writing">
					A read or search runs as soon as Claude finishes writing that call, before the rest of the reply arrives.
				</Feature>
			</div>
		</Section>

		<Section label="Simple">
			<div class="features">
				<Feature title="No plan mode">
					Say what you want. If clue heads the wrong way, type while it works to steer it.
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Output goes to your scrollback">
					The conversation prints like any other command's output. Each batch of tool calls shows as a short tree, and
					<code>ctrl+o</code> shows their output, diffs and errors.
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Built for modern terminals">
					Redraws don't flicker in terminals with synchronized output, such as Ghostty, kitty, WezTerm, iTerm2 and
					Alacritty. Where the terminal supports it, <code>/copy</code> works over SSH, file paths are clickable, and you
					can jump between your prompts.
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="/copy takes a request">
					<code>/copy the sql</code> copies only that part of the last reply. A small model picks the part.
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Three permission modes">
					<code>auto</code> runs routine work and has risky actions reviewed first. <code>ask</code> asks before each
					action. <code>full-access</code> runs every action without checks.
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="A second model for hard steps">
					Pick a pair in <code>/model</code>, such as <code>sonnet 5.5 + opus</code>. Sonnet does the work, and asks Opus
					before it plans a change or when it's stuck. Each reply's cost includes both.
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Your existing instructions">
					clue reads <code>AGENTS.md</code> and <code>CLAUDE.md</code> files, so a project set up for Claude Code works as
					it is.
				</Feature>
			</div>
		</Section>

		<Section label="Get started">
			<Steps steps={steps} />
			<p class="note">The <a href="/docs">quick start</a> covers other ways to sign in, and what to do if something looks wrong.</p>
		</Section>
	</main>
</Base>
